  "title": "tls: fix dissection of decrypted handshake messages",
  "description": "`dissect_tls_handshake` uses `is_encrypted_handshake_message` to determine\nwhether the message is encrypted. When an unfragmented handshake message\n(such as Finished) occurs after a Change Cipher Spec message, the logic\nin that function assumes that the message is encrypted, so\n`dissect_tls_handshake` refrains from dissecting it further.\n\nHowever, this logic doesn't make sense when the message had already been\ndecrypted by Wireshark (which is the case when `maybe_encrypted` is false).\nIn this case, it couldn't possibly still be encrypted, but Wireshark refuses\nto dissect it anyway.\n\nFix this by only calling `is_encrypted_handshake_message` when `maybe_encrypted`\nis true. This also makes passing `maybe_encrypted` to\n`is_encrypted_handshake_message` pointless, so remove that parameter.\n\nNote that this fix is only relevant for TLS 1.2 and older, since in TLS 1.3\nChange Cipher Spec is a no-op.",
